Western secularism is essentially a STRICT WALL OF SEPARATION between state and religion. The state stays out of religious affairs; religion stays out of public life. The US First Amendment forbids the establishment of any religion; the French principle of laïcité keeps religious symbols out of public schools.

INDIAN SECULARISM by contrast is one of PRINCIPLED DISTANCE and EQUAL RESPECT (sarva dharma sambhava). The Indian state:
 - Has NO official religion;
 - Respects ALL religions equally;
 - Provides FREEDOM of religion to every citizen;
 - But also can INTERVENE in religious matters to reform unjust practices — banning untouchability, opening temples to all castes, regulating temple endowments, prohibiting child marriage. The state can also subsidise or aid religious institutions (e.g. religious minority schools).

The Indian model recognises that in a deeply religious society, total separation is neither possible nor desirable; instead, the state must engage with all religions equally, and may even reform them when basic rights are at stake. Indian secularism is therefore more positive and engaged than its western counterparts.
